|> Exercise
    handle=_cosine_rule_geometric_3_

    Find expressions for areas A—F and use these to
    give a formula for $c$ in terms of $a$, $b$ and
    $\theta_{ab}$:
    !! Find expressions for the
    !! areas A, B, C, D below that allow you to find
    !! a formula for $c$ in terms of 
    !! all variables that do not contain “$c$” in their name:

    |> Image
        !! src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_statement.svg
        src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_statement_v2.svg

    |> Solution

        Keeping in mind that

        $$
        \cos(2\eta - x) = -\cos x
        $$

        for all $x\in \rr$,
        and that, in particular,

        $$
        \cos(2\eta - \theta_{ab}) = -\cos\theta_{ab}
        $$

        is a positive number in this diagram since
        $0 < 2\eta - \theta_{ab} < \eta$ is an acute angle, 
        the areas are:

        |> Image
            src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_sol1_v2.svg

        !! |> ImageLeft
        !!     line=0.5
        !!     src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_sol1_cloud.svg
        !!     offset-x=-28%
        !!     offset-y=-2em

        Then

        $$
        \Large c = \sqrt{c^2} = \sqrt{\te{D} + \te{C}} = \sqrt{\te{E} + \te{F}} = \sqrt{(a^2 + \te{B}) + (b^2 + \te{A})} = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos\theta_{ab}}
        $$

        where we use the fact that

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        \te{D} = \te{E}\, \\
        \up{1.4}\te{C} = \te{F}\,
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        in the third equality, and the fact that

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        \te{E} = a^2 + \te{B}\, \\
        \up{1.4}\te{F} = b^2 + \te{A}\,
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        in the fourth equality, and the fact that

        $$
        \te{A} = \te{B} = -ab\cos\theta_{ab}
        $$

        in the last equality.
        
        So we obtain the same formula as in Exercise >>_cosine_rule_geometric_2_!
        !! I.e.,

        !! |> Boxed

        !!     $$
        !!     \Large c = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos\theta_{ab}}
        !!     $$

        !! ...the same formula as in Exercise >>_cosine_rule_geometric_2_.

        !! Since

        !! $$
        !! \cos(2\eta - x) = -\cos x
        !! $$

        !! for all $x \in \rr$, and

        !! $$
        !! \cos(2\eta - \theta_{ab}) = -\cos \theta_{ab}
        !! $$

        !! in particular, we have these orange
        !! measurements & accompanying purple areas (A & B):
        
        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_sol1.svg

        !! |> ImageLeft
        !!     src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_cloud.svg
        !!     offset-y=44%

        !! Secondly we have these measurements and areas, giving us two
        !! more different ways of writing A and B, as well:

        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/_e5_cosine_rule_geometric_3_sol2.svg

        !! Thus

        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! \te{A} = \te{D} = bc\cos\theta_{bc} = b^2 - ab\cos\theta_{ab}, \\
        !! \up{1.4}\te{B} = \te{C} = ac\cos\theta_{ac} = a^2 - ab\cos\theta_{ab}\,
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        !! summarizing the two figures, and

        !! $$
        !! \Large
        !! c = \sqrt{c^2} = \sqrt{\te{C} + \te{D}} = \sqrt{\te{B} + \te{A}} = \sqrt{b^2 - ab\cos\theta_{ab} + a^2 - ab\cos\theta_{ab}} = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos\theta_{ab}}
        !! $$

        !! where we use $\te{C} = \te{B}$, $\te{D} = \te{A}$ in the third
        !! equality, or

        !! |> Boxed

        !!     $$
        !!     \Large c = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos\theta_{ab}}
        !!     $$

        !! in conclusion, which is the same formula as obtained in Exercise >>_cosine_rule_geometric_2_!